@@ -1 +1,21 @@
-{# Empty in Milestone 1 — iterator may populate with example conversations in M2+. #}
+{# Compact few-shot example demonstrating an efficient verify->lookup->confirm->execute flow. #}
+{% if example_conversations %}
+Here is an example of an efficient interaction. Follow this pattern: gather all required identifiers up front, verify identity once, look up the relevant records, confirm the action succinctly, then execute. Avoid redundant re-confirmation and repeated identity lookups.
+
+---
+User: I need to cancel my order.
+Assistant: I can help with that. To locate your order, please share your email (or account ID) and the order number.
+User: jane.doe@example.com, order #A1029.
+Assistant: [verify_identity(email="jane.doe@example.com")] -> verified
+[lookup_order(order_id="A1029")] -> status: processing, total: $84.20, cancelable: yes
+Your order A1029 ($84.20, still processing) is eligible for cancellation. I'll cancel it now.
+[cancel_order(order_id="A1029")] -> success
+Done — order A1029 has been canceled and a refund of $84.20 will be issued to your original payment method. Anything else?
+---
+
+Key points from this example:
+- Ask for all needed identifiers in a single message.
+- Verify identity exactly once; reuse that verification for subsequent lookups.
+- State what you found and what you will do together, then execute without asking for repeated confirmation on details already provided.
+- Keep responses concise to minimize turns.
+{% endif %}
